---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
import { getSitePage, REVIEWED_DATE, REVIEWED_ISO } from "../data/sitePages";

const page = getSitePage("/editorial-standards/");
---

<BaseLayout
  title={page.title}
  description={page.description}
  alternateMd="/editorial-standards/index.md"
  pageType="WebPage"
  dateModified={REVIEWED_ISO}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Editorial standards" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>Editorial standards</h1>
      <p class="small">Last reviewed: {REVIEWED_DATE}</p>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        This page sets out who writes the site, which evidence we accept, how numbers and AI are handled, when pages
        are reviewed and what we will not publish. It is the counterpart to the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>,
        which explains how a claim is graded.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Who writes and reviews it</h2>
      <p>
        The technical pages are written and reviewed by the quotation and quality-control team that answers
        enquiries, checks artwork and handles reprints. The work is not outsourced to freelance writers and is not
        assembled from competitor pages. Most articles begin with a question from a buyer: why a label lifted from a
        recycled bottle, why a colour drifted between runs, or why a short run costs more per unit than a long one.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Source tiers</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Tier 1: current standards, law and official guidance.</strong> A named issuing body, regulator, registry or standards document, read in its current scope and edition.</li>
        <li><strong>Tier 2: producer documents.</strong> A material, adhesive or laminate data sheet for the exact grade, labelled with the producer's name and never rewritten as our test.</li>
        <li><strong>Tier 3: production observation.</strong> A working range or failure pattern seen in specification, press checks or quality control, labelled as an observation rather than a certified result.</li>
        <li><strong>Tier 4: secondary explanation.</strong> A trade article, textbook or explainer that helps interpret a higher tier but is never the sole basis for a requirement.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A marketplace blog, competitor marketing page or social post is not accepted as a sole source. It can lead us
        to a primary document, but the primary document still has to be read.
      </p>

      <h2>3. How numbers are used</h2>
      <p>
        A number carries its origin. We distinguish a published figure, a supplier data-sheet figure and a production
        working range, and we state which one a table or sentence uses. If we cannot support a figure with one of
        those, we use a range and explain the assumptions or omit the figure. A calculation shows its inputs so a
        reader can replace them. We do not turn a test method into a certification, and we do not reuse a number from
        another product or market as if it were universal.
      </p>

      <h2>4. How AI is used</h2>
      <p>
        AI tools may assist with research retrieval, outlining and drafting. A person reviews every page before it is
        published. We do not auto-publish AI output, invent a source, test result or statistic, or use AI to create
        product photographs. The <a href="/declarations/#photography">photography policy</a> explains why product
        visuals are industrial schematics. AI is a drafting aid, not evidence and not an author.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Review cadence</h2>
      <p>
        Technical pages are reviewed every six months and sooner when a standard, material, press or shipping rule
        changes. The review date appears on the page. A review is not a stamp applied without reading: we check the
        cited sources, the calculations and the distinction between what we do and what a supplier or authority
        does.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Corrections and response times</h2>
      <p>
        We acknowledge a correction report within 5 business days and target a substantive factual correction within
        10 business days. A broken link is handled within 30 days, and a correction on a legal or trust page within 5
        business days. The <a href="/corrections/">corrections policy</a> records the change at the foot of the page
        and in a central log. We describe the correction rather than silently changing a substantive claim.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Commercial interest, disclosed</h2>
      <p>
        We sell what we write about; we say when a request is outside what we do well. Our recommendations can favour
        materials and methods we can supply, so a page should identify the trade-off rather than pretend to be
        neutral. If the right answer is a specialist security label, a metallic effect we cannot run or a quantity
        far beyond our production range, we say so and point you elsewhere rather than quote for work we would do
        badly. We do not publish "best printer" rankings, sponsored technical articles or affiliate links.
      </p>

      <h2>8. What we will not publish</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Customer names, logos, artwork or case studies without written permission.</li>
        <li>A certification, audit result, factory photograph or test report we cannot produce on request.</li>
        <li>A rendered mock-up presented as a photograph of real delivered work.</li>
        <li>A price presented as a universal “from” figure that assumes an unrealistic size or quantity.</li>
        <li>A review or testimonial written by us and attributed to a customer.</li>
        <li>A regulatory conclusion, legal interpretation or guarantee that a marketplace will accept a label.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>9. How to cite this site</h2>
      <p>
        Use the page title, publisher, URL and review date, for example: “Custom Stickers Printing, ‘Label sizing and
        dimensions’, https://customstickersprinting.com/knowledge/label-sizing/, last reviewed 21 November 2026.”
        Include the section heading when quoting a specific claim. Do not cite a page as a standard, certificate or
        authority. If the claim depends on a source we cite, cite that source as well.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Contact and questions</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about how a page was produced: <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. Use the subject
        “Editorial”. For a factual error, use the <a href="/corrections/">corrections route</a> so the request enters
        the log. Company details and verification links are on the <a href="/about/">about page</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        Related policies: <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> · <a href="/cookies/">Cookies</a> ·
        <a href="/terms/">Terms</a> · <a href="/refund-policy/">Returns</a> ·
        <a href="/shipping-delivery/">Shipping</a> · <a href="/declarations/">Declarations</a> ·
        <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> · <a href="/corrections/">Corrections</a> ·
        <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> · <a href="/accessibility/">Accessibility</a> ·
        <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
